theory.intro:
The **absolute value** (or **magnitude**) of a real number $x$, written $|x|$, is its distance from zero on a number line. Because distance is never negative, $|x| \ge 0$ for all real numbers $x$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[thick, <->] (-3.2,0) -- (3.2,0);
\draw[thick] (-2,-0.15) -- (-2,0.15);
\draw[thick] (0,-0.15) -- (0,0.15);
\draw[thick] (2,-0.15) -- (2,0.15);
\node[below] at (-2,-0.15) {$-x$};
\node[below] at (0,-0.15) {$0$};
\node[below] at (2,-0.15) {$x$};
\draw[<->] (-2,0.6) -- (0,0.6) node[midway, above] {$|-x|$};
\draw[<->] (0,0.6) -- (2,0.6) node[midway, above] {$|x|$};
\draw[dashed] (-2,0.15) -- (-2,0.6);
\draw[dashed] (0,0.15) -- (0,0.6);
\draw[dashed] (2,0.15) -- (2,0.6);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
